<header class="sheet-header flexrow">
	{{#if stonetop.playbook.img}}
	<img class="profile-img stonetop-header-playbook-icon" src="{{stonetop.playbook.img}}"
	     alt="{{stonetop.playbook.name}}" title="{{stonetop.playbook.name}}">
	{{else}}
	{{!-- data-edit belongs on the <img>, never on the button: FormDataExtended reads [data-edit]
	      elements into the submit payload, taking an IMG's src but any other element's innerHTML —
	      which the img FilePathField then rejects, blocking every submit (including a rename). --}}
	<button type="button" class="stonetop-image-btn" data-action="editImage" aria-label="{{actor.name}}"><img data-edit="img" class="profile-img" src="{{actor.img}}" alt="{{actor.name}}" title="{{actor.name}}"></button>
	{{/if}}
	<div class="header-fields">
		{{!-- Who you are, and what you are: "Anwen, The Would-be Hero". The steading's masthead writes
		      the same sentence with its size pill — a category reads with the name, not in a row of
		      numbers — and the playbook is the character's.

		      IN the h1, so the heading is the whole sentence rather than the name with a caption
		      beside it.

		      The field is quiet until you reach for it (see the CSS): a name is typed once, and at
		      rest this line is a title, not a form. --}}
		<h1 class="charname">
			<input name="name" type="text" value="{{actor.name}}"
			       aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.actorName'}}"
			       placeholder="Name"
			       {{#unless editable}}disabled{{/unless}}>
			{{!-- Characters only: this masthead is the NPC card's too, and an NPC has no playbook. --}}
			{{#if stonetop.playbook}}
			<span class="stonetop-charname-playbook">{{stonetop.playbook.title}}</span>
			{{/if}}
		</h1>

		{{!-- What you are suffering from, under your own name — and where you mark it.

		      The masthead is the one block the fold does not take, and the crest beside the name is
		      taller than the name field, so there is a line of room here that exists whether anything
		      uses it or not. Folded, that is where the debilities go: the band's three bracketed ticks
		      are off the sheet, and "which ones do I have" is the first thing a hindered stat's red
		      number makes you ask.

		      The steading's ledger line, exactly: all three in the book's order so nothing moves when
		      you mark one, a circle you can click, the name, and — for the ones you HAVE — what it does
		      after a dash. An unmarked one keeps its name and drops its sentence: three paragraphs of
		      rules prose under a name field is a wall, and the two you do not have are not the question
		      being asked.

		      The FIRST sentence of it — what the debility is, not which rolls it hinders. The clause
		      that names the stats is already on the sheet twice over: those two numbers are the red
		      ones on the line directly below this.

		      Marking works from here because the tick is the same control the band's is: one
		      `data-change-action="debility"`, one handler, no second path to keep in step.

		      Rendered only where the context carries debilities: `actor-header.hbs` is the NPC card's
		      masthead too, and an NPC has none. --}}
		{{#if stonetop.debilities}}
		<ul class="stonetop-masthead-debilities stonetop-unmarked"
		    aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.debilities'}}">
			{{#each stonetop.debilities}}
			<li class="stonetop-masthead-debility{{#if active}} is-active{{/if}}">
				<label class="stonetop-masthead-debility-control">
					<input type="checkbox" class="stonetop-masthead-debility-check"
					       data-change-action="debility" data-slug="{{key}}"
					       {{#if active}}checked{{/if}}>
					<span class="stonetop-masthead-debility-circle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
					<span class="stonetop-masthead-debility-name">{{name}}</span>
					<span class="stonetop-masthead-debility-effect"><span aria-hidden="true">—</span> {{summary}}</span>
				</label>
			</li>
			{{/each}}
		</ul>
		{{/if}}
	</div>
</header>
